"""Original educational material for observed design intents and excluded legacy guides."""
GUIDES=[]
def add(route,title,sections,related,fields):GUIDES.append(dict(route=route,title=title,sections=sections,related=related,fields=fields))
add('/blog/airbnb-outdoor-space-design.html','Airbnb outdoor-space design: plan the guest routine',[
('Map the complete outdoor visit','Begin at the door guests use to enter the outdoor space. Walk the route with a tray, shoes and a bag, then identify where guests eat, relax and store items. Record the actual surfaces, changes in level, drainage observations and available lighting. Do not choose furniture from an isolated patio photograph before understanding the route. A comfortable seating group still needs usable access and a place for the things people bring outside.'),
('Separate use zones from the photography composition','Place dining, lounging and activity areas on a measured plan. Show chairs in use, doors open and storage accessible. Check whether one activity interrupts another: smoke or cooking activity near a lounge, noisy play beside a bedroom, or a dining chair blocking the only route. Use the intended guest group to test the arrangement. A small property may be better served by one complete outdoor function than by several incomplete ones.'),
('Specify exposure and reset','Record sun, rain, wind and storage conditions as property observations. Ask suppliers for the actual product’s intended-use and care information. Decide who moves or secures cushions and loose items when needed, and where they go. Avoid specifying a complicated daily reset that the operating team cannot perform. Include furniture feet, protective accessories and suitable storage in the inventory rather than treating them as incidental purchases.'),
('Worked plan: a compact group patio','In a hypothetical six-person rental, the initial concept places a large lounge and dining set on a narrow patio. The meal test shows that occupied chairs prevent access to the door. The owner chooses a complete dining zone and a smaller secondary sitting area, with storage at the side of the route. The revised plan is checked with all seats occupied before ordering. No revenue increase is assumed; the improvement is a usable outdoor meal and an unobstructed route.'),
('Accept the installed setting','Repeat the arrival, seating, meal and evening-light tests after installation. Follow product instructions and obtain qualified help for electrical or other specialist work. Check stability and condition before guest use, and document any unresolved issues. Ask the cleaning team to reset the area from a photograph and report what takes extra handling. Update listing photographs only after the agreed arrangement is complete.'),
('Maintain a weather and replacement record','Keep product identifiers, finishes, care instructions and storage arrangements with the room inventory. Record damage or recurring movement rather than assuming a decorative accessory is suitable because it survived one dry day. Decide what happens when an essential seat is unavailable and how a replacement preserves the usable capacity. Review the setup when operating routines or guest capacity change.')],['/learn/wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan/','/learn/reference/outdoors/','/resources/guides/guest-capacity-furnishing-matrix/'],['Occupied-chair route test','Weather exposure and storage','Outdoor reset responsibility'])
add('/blog/best-throw-blankets-for-vacation-rental/','Throw blankets for vacation rentals: care and replacement',[
('Choose the role before the material','Decide whether the blanket is primarily a guest comfort item, a styling accent or both. Specify where it belongs and how guests can identify it. The item needs to suit the actual seating or sleeping arrangement without making the cleaner guess whether it has been used. Avoid buying a large number of decorative textiles before the team has a workable cleaning and storage plan.'),
('Read care information before buying in quantity','Compare the exact care label with the property’s laundry equipment and operating routine. Ask how washing, drying and any permitted stain treatment will be handled. A fiber description alone does not establish that a blanket can use the same cycle as towels or bedding. Test an actual sample using its permitted method, then inspect appearance, feel, dimensions and drying condition before ordering duplicates.'),
('Test the styling arrangement during reset','Place the blanket where guests will use it and ask the cleaner to recreate that arrangement. Count how many handling decisions the display requires. A simple folded position may be easier to reset than an elaborate drape with several accessories. Make sure the blanket does not hide a damaged surface or compensate for inadequate seating comfort. Styling should clarify the guest experience rather than conceal an unfinished room.'),
('Worked trial: a pet-friendly living room','A hypothetical host selects a textured accent blanket for a pet-friendly rental. The sample trial shows that visible hair takes too long to remove under the allowed care routine. The owner tests a different suitable material and keeps the color direction through a smaller accent elsewhere. The decision follows the sample’s actual maintenance performance, not a general assertion that one fiber is always best for pets.'),
('Plan spares and condition checks','Assign a replacement location and keep the spare separately identifiable from used textiles. Record the exact product and variant so a substitute can be evaluated for size, color and care. Agree how the team reports pulls, stains, odor or other condition problems. Replace based on observed condition and the applicable care outcome, rather than an unsupported universal month-by-month schedule.'),
('Describe what the guide can establish','This is a selection and trial method, not a product ranking based on independent laundry tests. Use seller information as an input, then verify the actual sample and the operating fit. Keep receipts and trial notes together so the owner can understand why an item was retained or rejected. A lower purchase price is useful only when the complete maintenance and replacement arrangement also works.')],['/learn/pet-friendly-furnishing-system/','/learn/turnover-time-design-study/','/learn/reference/surfaces/'],['Care-label requirements','Sample trial and drying outcome','Reset position and spare location'])
add('/blog/fishing-lodge-interior-design.html','Fishing-lodge interiors: wet-gear and group-use design',[
('Follow the return from an outing','Write the arrival routine before choosing the style. In a hypothetical fishing-oriented stay, guests may return with wet outerwear, equipment and food. Map where those items go before people sit on upholstered furniture or walk into bedrooms. Record the owner’s rules for equipment and cleaning. A useful brief defines the guest routine without assuming every fishing property has the same storage, climate or operating policy.'),
('Create a clear gear boundary','Measure the entry, storage and circulation together. Separate guest belongings from owner supplies and identify where wet items can be handled under the property’s actual maintenance plan. Ask the responsible professionals about unresolved moisture or drainage conditions rather than trying to solve them with decor. Do not place equipment storage where it obstructs the guest route or access to household controls.'),
('Coordinate group meals and resting areas','Test the shared rooms at the advertised group capacity. A lodge may have enough beds while lacking comfortable dining or places to rest between activities. Show the occupied dining arrangement and the route to the kitchen. Decide where guests can sit without moving equipment or unpacked bags. Plan lighting for meal preparation and reading as distinct tasks instead of relying on a single atmospheric fixture.'),
('Worked brief: an entry that protects the lounge','For a hypothetical four-bedroom lodge, the owner keeps the main lounge furniture but observes damp jackets and bags arriving there after outings. The proposed refresh adds a defined entry-storage routine and changes the placement of a small bench so the route remains open. The owner tests it with the intended group’s belongings before buying more lounge accessories. The example addresses an observed routine; it is not a documented client result.'),
('Choose a local visual language carefully','Use a limited material and color direction that fits the actual rooms and retained pieces. If art references fishing or the landscape, choose a few intentional locations rather than filling usable surfaces with props. Inspect cleaning access around decorative objects and avoid suggesting that an item is authentic local history without evidence. Keep the room welcoming for guests whose interests differ within the same group.'),
('Document the operating handoff','Photograph the intended storage arrangement and give cleaners a clear reset reference. Record finish care, spare-item locations and which conditions require owner attention. Review the brief after observing real turnovers. If guests consistently put equipment in another location, investigate whether the storage is hard to find, inconvenient or undersized before adding more signage.')],['/learn/wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan/','/learn/large-group-activity-zoning/','/learn/reference/arrival/'],['Return-from-outing routine','Gear boundary and storage capacity','Group meal and lounge test'])
add('/blog/modern-airbnb-design/','Modern Airbnb design: a functional room system',[
('Define modern through choices','Translate the style goal into a limited set of decisions: furniture proportions, material relationships, lighting and the amount of visible decoration. Avoid treating modern as a requirement to make every surface pale, every chair sculptural or every room empty. Start with the building, retained furniture and guest routines. A modern scheme can remain warm and practical when its choices form a coherent system.'),
('Use the measured layout as the foundation','Draw the actual room and place the essential functions first. Check the usable seating, dining, storage and workspace before choosing accent pieces. A low-profile sofa can look proportionate in a photograph while still needing an appropriate seated-use test. Evaluate the actual item with the intended guests and route, rather than assuming a visual style guarantees comfort or accessibility.'),
('Build contrast without increasing reset work','Choose where contrast matters: a large material change, a defined art location or one deliberate accent. Keep small objects away from surfaces needed for cooking, working and unpacking. Ask the cleaner to reset a sample arrangement and note ambiguous placements. A coherent room should not depend on many fragile accessories or a sequence of styling decisions after every stay.'),
('Worked scheme: a retained wood table','A hypothetical urban rental keeps a substantial wood dining table. The new plan uses simpler lighting and a limited upholstery palette to connect the dining and lounge areas. The owner tests chair proportions and tabletop care before selecting decor. Keeping the existing table becomes an intentional design constraint rather than a reason to replace the whole room. The case is illustrative, with no claimed booking improvement.'),
('Test the design under ordinary use','Walk the room after dark and during a realistic meal or work session. Check whether switches are discoverable, useful surfaces remain clear and storage has enough room for guest belongings. Review materials with the cleaning team using the actual instructions. Finish issues that are hidden by a styled image should be recorded and corrected before photography.'),
('Make the scheme replaceable','Keep a concise specification of dimensions, materials, finishes and visual priorities. Define what a substitute must preserve if an item is discontinued. This is more useful than a mood board alone because it lets later purchases maintain the room’s function and relationships. Review the specification whenever retained pieces or the guest capacity change.')],['/learn/reference/identity/','/learn/studio-rental-zoning-without-extra-furniture/','/blog/airbnb-photography-guide.html'],['Furniture proportion and guest task','Material/contrast hierarchy','Substitute requirements'])
add('/blog/art-deco-color-palette-guide.html','Art Deco color palettes: design a maintainable rental',[
('Choose the palette hierarchy','Start with the largest retained surfaces and decide which color family will act as the background. Add a supporting material direction and a limited accent. A scheme inspired by Art Deco can use geometry, contrast and considered detail without requiring a collection of fragile statement pieces. State which visual relationship matters most so the design remains coherent when a product changes.'),
('Test reflective and patterned finishes together','Place samples beside the flooring, upholstery and window treatments in the actual room. Observe them in daylight and with the planned artificial lighting. Record glare, visible marks and how patterns compete at normal viewing distance. Evaluate the applicable care process before ordering. A finish that looks dramatic in a sample image may demand more cleaning or show wear differently in the property.'),
('Protect functional surfaces','Keep decorative geometry and accents away from areas guests need for meals, work and luggage. A patterned wall or a defined art location may carry the theme more effectively than many objects on a table. Check that the room remains understandable: guests should find switches and storage without interpreting a complicated decorative arrangement. Test the occupied layout as well as its empty composition.'),
('Worked sample board: dark accent and retained sofa','A hypothetical host wants a rich accent wall beside a serviceable neutral sofa. The sample board combines the proposed wall color, one metallic finish and a patterned textile. Under the room’s evening light the metallic surface produces unwanted glare, so the owner tests a less reflective alternative while retaining the color hierarchy. The decision is based on the room trial, not a universal rule about a finish.'),
('Specify the replaceable details','Record the exact color, finish and textile identifiers alongside care information. Note which elements are essential to the design and which can change within a defined range. Ask for appropriate instructions and qualified installation for mounted pieces. Keep the substitute process clear so a single damaged accessory does not force a full redesign.'),
('Review the completed room honestly','After installation, repeat the lighting and guest-use checks and photograph the actual room. Distinguish reference images and design concepts from completed property photographs. Avoid claims that a particular style inherently produces higher rates. Assess whether the chosen scheme supports the guest brief, the operating routine and a clear listing presentation.')],['/learn/reference/identity/','/blog/airbnb-photography-guide.html','/resources/damage-resistant-materials/'],['Background/support/accent hierarchy','Glare and pattern sample test','Finish and textile identifiers'])
add('/blog/airbnb-turnover-design-faster-cleaning/','Airbnb turnover design: observe, improve and retest',[
('Understand what turnover includes','Turnover is the sequence that prepares a property between guest stays. Its actual scope can include cleaning, laundry, inspection, restocking, reporting and resetting the room arrangement. Record the property’s agreed scope and team responsibilities before judging speed. A short visit that omits inspection is not equivalent to a complete reset. Design improvements should support a reliable finished condition.'),
('Observe the existing routine','Ask the operating team to walk through a normal reset and record tasks, locations and interruptions. Note avoidable trips for supplies, furniture that blocks access, unclear decorative placements and missing care information. Keep unusual events separate from routine work. One difficult turnover is useful evidence but does not establish the normal time for every stay.'),
('Choose one bottleneck at a time','Describe a specific problem and change one relevant condition. Move supplies closer to their use location, simplify an ambiguous display, or revise a storage layout only after confirming that the new arrangement still works for guests. Repeat the observation under comparable conditions. Do not claim a time saving from intuition alone or assume that removing all decor is the best operating choice.'),
('Worked observation: bedroom reset','In a hypothetical rental, the cleaner repeatedly leaves the bedroom to locate spare pillow protectors and an approved replacement lamp. The owner labels the spare storage and records compatible items in the room inventory. The next observation measures whether the trips were reduced while the final room still meets the agreed standard. The example illustrates a test design; it does not assert a universal number of minutes saved.'),
('Make the reset visible','Provide a clear reference photograph and a checklist of the items that should be present. Keep care instructions and reporting routes easy to find. A condition report should identify the room, item, problem and evidence, with a defined owner response. Decide what happens when an essential item is missing rather than leaving the cleaner to improvise an unsuitable substitute.'),
('Review quality alongside elapsed time','Evaluate the outcome as well as the clock: cleanliness, inventory, functioning lighting and an accurate room arrangement. A faster reset is useful only if it delivers the same agreed standard. Update the process after a furniture change, a new amenity or repeated reports. Keep observed results distinct from projected savings in a design proposal.')],['/learn/turnover-time-design-study/','/resources/guides/linen-par-stock-calculation/','/learn/reference/operations/'],['Turnover scope and final standard','Observed bottleneck and trial change','Comparable retest and quality outcome'])
add('/blog/scandinavian-color-palette-guide.html','Scandinavian color palettes for guest-ready rentals',[
('Start with the actual light and retained materials','A Scandinavian-inspired palette can be developed through restrained color, material texture and useful light, but the property’s existing surfaces should lead the decision. Record the flooring, cabinetry and retained furniture before choosing paint. A pale reference image photographed in different light cannot establish how the same color will read in your room. Keep the sample process tied to the property.'),
('Create warmth through relationships','Choose a background and a small set of supporting materials rather than adding many similar pale finishes without comparison. Test how wood tones, textiles and large furniture work together. Warmth is a design goal that should be judged in context; it does not require a particular paint name or a universal color-temperature prescription. Keep the room’s task lighting and storage needs visible throughout the process.'),
('Use contrast to clarify the room','Locate deliberate contrast at useful boundaries or a limited focal point. Check that important controls, storage and circulation remain easy to understand. Avoid a scheme that looks calm only when guest belongings are absent. Test where luggage, coats, drinks and work equipment will go, and reserve clear surfaces for those routines rather than filling them with styling objects.'),
('Worked palette: existing gray flooring','A hypothetical rental has cool gray flooring and a retained oak table. Instead of buying white furniture to copy a reference, the owner tests a warm neutral background and a muted textile beside both existing materials. Samples are reviewed in daylight and after dark. The selected direction must work across the shared room and support its cleaning routine. This is an illustrative planning exercise, not a documented customer result.'),
('Check care before expanding the scheme','Review the exact instructions for the proposed textiles and finishes. Test a sample with the allowed cleaning method, then inspect it under the room’s light. Record which materials show marks or require handling the team cannot provide. A quiet visual palette should not depend on replacing large items whenever a small stain or finish mismatch appears.'),
('Preserve the intent when products change','Document the relationships that matter: light/dark balance, texture, furniture scale and the limited accent direction. Keep exact finish identifiers where consistency is necessary and acceptable ranges where substitutions are possible. Photograph the completed arrangement so future purchases can maintain the design without confusing an inspiration board with the installed room.')],['/blog/tropical-color-palette-guide.html','/learn/reference/identity/','/resources/damage-resistant-materials/'],['Existing flooring and wood-tone samples','Day/night palette comparison','Required relationships for substitutions'])
add('/blog/airbnb-bedroom-design-guide.html','Airbnb bedroom design: layout, storage and night use',[
('Brief the complete sleeping routine','Describe who the room is intended for and how they arrive, unpack, read, charge devices, sleep and leave. Record the actual doors, windows, controls and retained furniture. A bed count is one input, not a complete bedroom brief. Check where belongings go and whether the chosen arrangement supports the advertised sleeping configuration without moving essential furniture.'),
('Measure the installed arrangement','Use actual product dimensions and delivery information. Check door movement, storage access and the route around the bed with intended furniture in place. Consider the headboard, bedside items and window treatments together. Do not rely on a rendering to confirm dimensions or assume an item can reach the room because its assembled footprint fits. Identify who verifies site information before ordering.'),
('Commission after dark','Begin at the doorway with the room in its normal guest state. Find the controls, reach each sleeping position and test the lighting for reading and getting up at night. Check whether one guest’s routine unnecessarily disrupts another. Review privacy and window treatment operation under the actual conditions. Record the problem and obtain appropriate installation support for any changes that require it.'),
('Worked room: two different bedside experiences','In a hypothetical twin room, one bed has a convenient lamp and charging location while the other requires moving a nightstand to reach power. The owner revises the arrangement and repeats the task from both beds. The acceptance standard is equal usable support for the intended guests, not visual symmetry alone. The example does not establish a universal clearance or electrical solution.'),
('Connect bedding to laundry and spares','Record the bed and bedding sizes, product variants, care instructions and spare locations. Keep compatible replacements identifiable and distinguish clean spares from used textiles. Coordinate the quantity with the property’s laundry plan rather than adopting a universal set count without observing the routine. Ask cleaners how missing or damaged items will be reported and replaced.'),
('Accept and photograph the finished room','Resolve condition issues and the punch list before the listing shoot. Show the actual sleeping arrangement and describe any flexible configuration accurately. Keep installation instructions, finish care and approved substitutes with the room record. Repeat the commissioning check when a bed, window treatment or control changes, because a small substitution can alter the guest routine.')],['/learn/rental-bedroom-nighttime-commissioning/','/resources/guides/linen-par-stock-calculation/','/learn/reference/sleep/'],['Sleeping configuration and unpacking','Both bedside night-use tests','Bedding sizes and compatible spares'])
add('/blog/airbnb-bathroom-design-guide.html','Airbnb bathroom design: plan a practical refresh',[
('Separate condition issues from decoration','Begin with observed condition and the guest routine. Record recurring moisture, damaged surfaces, poor storage or difficult cleaning before choosing new colors and accessories. A finish refresh does not establish that the cause of dampness or another building issue has been resolved. Obtain appropriate assessment for unresolved conditions and keep decorative work separate from that investigation.'),
('Map the guest sequence','Walk through arriving with toiletries, using the mirror, placing clothing, bathing, drying towels and leaving the room. Identify where personal items go and whether storage or accessories obstruct the routine. Test the space for the intended guest group. The goal is a usable arrangement with clear care responsibilities, not a count of decorative objects on the vanity.'),
('Define the construction and furnishing boundary','If the project includes plumbing, electrical, waterproofing or other building work, identify the responsible qualified parties and required approvals. Separate those scopes from mirrors, storage accessories, towels and styling. Ask who supplies, installs and checks each interface. Product selection should follow verified site conditions and the applicable instructions, rather than a design image alone.'),
('Worked refresh: keep the vanity, change the routine','A hypothetical owner retains a sound vanity but observes cluttered counters and towels that have no clear drying location. The brief asks for improved storage and a suitable towel arrangement before optional accent finishes. The owner checks the proposed locations and care plan with the operating team. This is a planning example; it does not diagnose moisture or certify that a proposed installation is appropriate.'),
('Test care and reset','Use samples and the actual product instructions to understand finish care. Ask the cleaner to reset the completed arrangement and identify areas that are difficult to reach or ambiguous to stage. Keep guest supplies easy to locate and owner replacements clearly separated. Record defects and unresolved conditions before accepting the refresh or taking new listing photographs.'),
('Keep evidence for future work','Document retained items, new purchases, installation descriptions and completion records. Save product and finish identifiers so later repairs can be scoped accurately. Photograph the final storage arrangement and update the room checklist when an accessory changes. Review repeated operating reports as evidence of a design or maintenance problem rather than assuming new decor will solve it.')],['/learn/bathroom-refresh-design-brief/','/resources/guides/guest-towel-drying-plan/','/learn/reference/bathroom/'],['Observed condition and assessment needed','Guest toiletries/towel routine','Trade and furnishing scope boundary'])
add('/blog/how-to-decorate-airbnb-on-a-budget/','Decorate an Airbnb on a budget: prioritize useful changes',[
('Define what the budget must finish','List the rooms, retained furniture and required guest functions before selecting accessories. Decide whether the assignment is a focused refresh or a complete setup. A fixed spending limit needs a defined finished condition; otherwise small purchases can consume the budget while essential seating, lighting or storage remains unresolved. Keep the intended guest and operating routine in the brief.'),
('Use a keep, repair, replace inventory','Inspect each existing item and record its condition, function and compatibility with the plan. Keep useful pieces when they fit the brief, price a specific repair when appropriate, and replace items that cannot meet the required use. Do not assume that every mismatched accessory justifies a large furniture purchase. Test the room arrangement around retained pieces before shopping.'),
('Compare complete costs','For each proposed purchase, include quantity, item price, taxes, delivery, assembly, mounting and any other necessary work. Keep design fees and local execution costs separate so they are visible. A discount on the product may be outweighed by additional logistics or an unsuitable return policy. Track committed spending and unresolved estimates without treating unknown costs as zero.'),
('Worked allocation: a focused lounge refresh','A hypothetical owner has a $2,400 planning cap. They reserve $900 for an essential furniture replacement, $450 for lighting, $300 for delivery and setup, $250 for permitted finish work and $500 as uncommitted room for known uncertainties. These invented amounts illustrate allocation only; they are not market quotes. The owner replaces them with actual proposals before approving any order.'),
('Sequence purchases by dependencies','Resolve measurements and the largest functional decisions first. Test materials and confirm delivery arrangements before buying coordinating accessories in quantity. Approve substitutions against the same room requirements and recheck the total when something changes. If funds become tight, defer an optional styling layer rather than leaving an essential task half completed.'),
('Evaluate the outcome without a revenue promise','Accept the room through a guest-use and turnover walkthrough. Record what the changes solved, what remains and the final cost. If performance is later measured, distinguish design changes from season, pricing and management changes. A budget refresh can be worthwhile because it improves a specific problem, even when no credible forecast of additional bookings is available.')],['/resources/guides/keep-repair-replace-audit/','/blog/diy-airbnb-makeover-before-after-strategies/','/resources/guides/quote-scope-comparison/'],['Finished scope and retained inventory','Committed versus unresolved costs','Optional work that can be deferred'])
add('/blog/roi-of-professional-str-design.html','STR design ROI: build a transparent scenario',[
('Define the investment and the comparison','A design ROI calculation needs a clear project cost, comparison period and definition of benefit. Separate design fees, furnishings, delivery, installation, related work and downtime. State whether the analysis concerns gross revenue, operating contribution or cash flow. These are different measures, and using one in the numerator while describing another can make a result misleading.'),
('Separate a projection from an observed result','Before a project, additional revenue is an assumption to test, not an outcome. After a project, compare equivalent periods where possible and record other changes in pricing, availability, management or amenities. A before-and-after total alone does not establish that design caused the difference. Keep the original assumptions so the owner can see which parts of the plan were accurate.'),
('Worked contribution scenario','Suppose a hypothetical project costs $18,000. The owner tests an assumed $6,000 annual increase in gross revenue and $1,800 in additional operating costs, giving $4,200 of additional annual contribution. Under those assumptions, simple annual contribution divided by project cost is about 23.3%, and simple payback is about 4.3 years. These figures are illustrative, exclude financing and tax effects, and do not predict a property’s results.'),
('Test the downside and the operating workload','Repeat the scenario with a smaller benefit, delayed opening and higher ongoing costs. Include supplies, maintenance or labor required by a new amenity where relevant. If the conclusion depends on an optimistic rate increase, show that dependence explicitly. A design choice should also be evaluated for guest function, maintenance and budget fit rather than relying entirely on an uncertain return estimate.'),
('Use evidence when reviewing provider claims','Request the definition of the reported metric, the properties included, the dates and the comparison method. Ask what else changed at the same time. Distinguish a projected listing estimate from realized revenue and a selected case from a representative sample. An attractive portfolio and a headline percentage do not replace a transparent explanation of the evidence.'),
('Keep financial records separate from the design forecast','Save invoices, item locations, installation descriptions and completion dates. Discuss property-specific tax and accounting treatment with the responsible professionals rather than embedding an assumed tax saving in the design pitch. Review actual operating results after a defined period and update future decisions using what happened, including projects that did not meet the original assumptions.')],['/resources/guides/furniture-cost-per-use/','/design-roadmap/tax-and-cost-segregation-records/','/blog/how-to-decorate-airbnb-on-a-budget/'],['Project-cost boundary','Assumed/observed benefit and time period','Operating costs and downside scenario'])
add('/blog/best-couch-for-airbnb-2026.html','Airbnb couch specifications: capacity, care and replacement',[
('Specify the actual seating requirement','Start with the advertised guest group and the way the lounge is used. Count usable seats in the complete arrangement rather than relying on a sofa’s marketing label. Check where guests place drinks, play games, watch a screen or talk. A larger couch is not automatically a better solution if it consumes circulation or leaves the rest of the room without useful surfaces.'),
('Measure the room and delivery route','Use the actual assembled and packaging information for the product under consideration. Check doors, stairs, turns and access restrictions with the responsible delivery team. Place the sofa and other furniture on the measured plan, then test the occupied arrangement. Confirm who verifies dimensions before ordering and how a product substitution will be approved if the original is unavailable.'),
('Evaluate upholstery with the operating team','Request the exact upholstery designation and applicable care information. Test a permitted cleaning method on an appropriate sample, and assess visible marks, drying needs and routine handling. Ask about replaceable components and compatible cushions where relevant. Do not assume a general label such as performance fabric or commercial grade establishes every care or warranty requirement for your intended use.'),
('Worked choice: sectional or separate pieces','A hypothetical group rental can fit a sectional in the photograph but loses flexibility for conversation and cleaning access. The owner compares a smaller sofa with separately placed chairs using the same guest-capacity and reset tests. The choice follows the complete room use, product instructions and maintenance plan. This is an illustrative specification exercise, not an independent review of a particular model.'),
('Inspect before guest use','Record delivered condition, exact variant, assembly and any defects. Follow manufacturer instructions and obtain appropriate installation help where required. Test the actual seated positions and surrounding surfaces after setup. Keep packaging and condition evidence as needed for the agreed claims process, and resolve essential problems before treating the room as accepted.'),
('Prepare a replacement specification','Keep dimensions, upholstery, care guidance and required room functions together. Identify which aspects a substitute must preserve and who can approve it. Track observed condition rather than prescribing a universal replacement date or an arbitrary spending premium. A couch should be evaluated as part of the room’s ongoing use and maintenance, not only its purchase-day appearance.')],['/compare/slipcovered-vs-fixed-upholstery-sofa/','/learn/pet-friendly-furnishing-system/','/learn/reference/seating/'],['Occupied seating and room circulation','Exact upholstery and care trial','Packaging dimensions and delivery path'])
